{"chunk_id": "2038d3bb8338", "text": "This section recounts the famous moral test of King Usinara (often identified with the Shibi legend). A pigeon fleeing a hawk seeks refuge on the king\u2019s lap. The hawk argues that denying it the pigeon violates the natural order because predators must eat to live, and its own family will suffer if it starves. The king replies that protecting one who seeks shelter is a sacred duty, equal to the gravest moral obligations. The hawk proposes a compromise: the king may save the pigeon if he offers flesh from his own body equal to the bird\u2019s weight. Usinara agrees and cuts off pieces of his flesh, yet the pigeon still outweighs them. Finally, the king climbs onto the scale himself, demonstrating absolute commitment to dharma. The episode explores conflicting virtues\u2014compassion, duty, and natural law\u2014and dramatizes the ideal of self-sacrificial righteousness.\nKing Usinara refuses to surrender a pigeon seeking refuge and offers his own flesh to the hawk demanding it as rightful prey.\nThemes: dharma dilemma, sacrifice, protection of the helpless, conflicting duties, divine testing", "vector": [0.021723248064517975, -0.01991414651274681, -0.008000718429684639, 0.015019753947854042, -0.007839442230761051, 0.008708933368325233, -0.0214006956666708, -0.038117360323667526, -0.028651127591729164, -0.036238137632608414, 0.021779345348477364, -0.00030392740154638886, 0.02754322811961174, -0.007425732910633087, -0.0022385884076356888, -1.94747990462929e-05, 0.035368647426366806, -0.01799285225570202, 0.02065742015838623, -0.014038071036338806, 0.003332464024424553, 0.009830856695771217, -0.0141011793166399, -0.00046016403939574957, 0.0015864702872931957, 0.02344820648431778, 0.01873612590134144, -0.024485986679792404, 0.029254160821437836, -0.022536642849445343, 0.01583314873278141, -0.001240251585841179, 0.013477109372615814, -0.02351832576096058, -0.026603616774082184, 0.008807101286947727, 0.001921294373460114, -0.00046323